1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It is America's own version of the bullet train,

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
the Acela Express.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Its name was created from the words acceleration and excellence,

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
and many believe the Acela, with its state-of-the-art design,

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
power, and speed, could revolutionize the way we travel.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The Acela was the truly first new train developed in America.

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:34,880
developed in America in 30 years.

8
00:00:34,881 --> 00:00:39,140
So we're basically breaking ground.

9
00:00:39,141 --> 00:00:43,740
Launched in the winter of 2000 at a cost of $800 million,

10
00:00:43,741 --> 00:00:47,760
the Acela rockets between Boston and Washington, DC,

11
00:00:47,761 --> 00:00:53,100
at speeds reaching 150 miles an hour.

12
00:00:53,101 --> 00:00:56,260
The Acela takes about 2 and 1⁄2 hours to travel between New

13
00:00:56,261 --> 00:01:00,100
and Washington, and three hours and 18 minutes

14
00:01:00,101 --> 00:01:01,600
from New York to Boston.

15
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,200
The New York Boston leg is now about 90 minutes faster

16
00:01:06,201 --> 00:01:07,580
than earlier trains.

17
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:10,100
It has power to spare.

18
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:14,440
It's kind of the sports car of the industry right now.

19
00:01:14,441 --> 00:01:16,840
During the Acela's journey north of New York,

20
00:01:16,841 --> 00:01:18,620
there are so many twists and turns

21
00:01:18,621 --> 00:01:20,660
that if you were to fit them together,

22
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:23,860
they would create 10 full circles.

23
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:29,880
The Acela can take the turns at speeds higher than conventional trains because of its unique tilting cars.

24
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:37,820
As the Acela enters a curve, a computer in the lead car adjusts hydraulic jacks in the train's wheel sets.

25
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:46,660
As a result, the passenger cars will tilt by as much as four degrees depending on the speed of the train and sharpness of the curve.

26
00:01:46,661 --> 00:01:55,420
We have a series of transponders that are embedded in the track structure that actually

27
00:01:55,421 --> 00:02:00,660
give location to the train so the train knows what it's supposed to be doing at various

28
00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:01,660
locations.

29
00:02:01,661 --> 00:02:07,960
Without this technology, passengers would have to brace themselves for every turn.

30
00:02:07,961 --> 00:02:14,040
The ideal high-speed rail experience is you're in a railroad car, but you're not subject

31
00:02:14,041 --> 00:02:17,480
to an acute sensation of speed.

32
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:21,600
There's no drama associated with it.

33
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:25,480
The Acela belongs to an elite group

34
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:27,860
known as high-speed trains.

35
00:02:27,861 --> 00:02:30,220
It's a term roughly defined as passenger trains

36
00:02:30,221 --> 00:02:34,400
that travel above 200 kilometers or 125 miles an hour.

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,640
And like most high-speed trains,

38
00:02:41,641 --> 00:02:43,600
the Acela is electrically powered.

39
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,180
The benefit is that electric trains

40
00:02:46,181 --> 00:02:47,760
tend to be lighter in total weight

41
00:02:47,761 --> 00:02:51,100
than diesel locomotives, more economical to operate,

42
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,340
and they're able to achieve faster acceleration

43
00:02:54,341 --> 00:02:55,180
and stopping.

44
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,540
They acquire power from overhead wires

45
00:03:00,541 --> 00:03:01,780
called the catenary.

46
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:05,660
A current collector or pantograph carries electricity

47
00:03:05,661 --> 00:03:08,480
to an engine that drives the train's traction motors.

48
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:18,380
But as fast as the Acela is, it cannot travel at 150 miles an hour during its entire run.

49
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:26,040
If it could, the Boston to Washington trip might be closer to four or even three hours.

50
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:30,500
The principal culprit? It shares its route with other trains.

51
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:35,120
You have numerous areas where we have constrained operation, New York in particular.

52
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,300
Literally a thousand trains go in and out of there during the day.

53
00:03:39,301 --> 00:03:45,380
So anything that's moving a lot faster, a lot slower, tends to be an operational problem.

54
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:52,600
Thousands of people travel on the Acela trains each day, and the number is growing.

55
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:58,500
A one-way ticket from Boston to Washington is under $200.

56
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,380
Riders will find large windows that allow light to flood in,

57
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:09,280
oversized adjustable seats, ample legroom, and work tables with electrical outlets.

58
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,300
Ridership is excellent for us.

59
00:04:12,301 --> 00:04:15,300
We anticipate growth over the years,

60
00:04:15,301 --> 00:04:20,300
and certainly all the features that we've incorporated

61
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:25,300
in this particular train set will be used in train sets that will follow us.

62
00:04:25,301 --> 00:04:29,300
High-speed trains are among today's fastest land vehicles.

63
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:34,300
And yet it was not so long ago in human history

64
00:04:34,301 --> 00:04:38,300
that we could only go as fast as our feet or animals could carry us.

65
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:47,580
The ancient Greeks and later the Romans used what might be called the first railroads.

66
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:51,620
They built stone grooves for wagon wheels.

67
00:04:54,020 --> 00:05:02,220
By the late medieval period, German miners were using wooden tracks with rails and special wooden wheels to haul lead and tin from mines.

68
00:05:02,221 --> 00:05:07,280
A wheel, whether it's wood or metal or anything else,

69
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:12,500
traveling in the dirt requires a lot of effort to move.

70
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:17,080
The idea of building tracks spread to other parts of Europe,

71
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:20,140
and eventually rails and wheels were made of cast iron.

72
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,340
Then, in the late 1700s, came the invention

73
00:05:24,341 --> 00:05:26,980
that would one day revolutionize rail travel,

74
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:30,280
the steam engine.

75
00:05:30,281 --> 00:05:37,700
By the period of 1805 to 1810, it improved enough that boilers could get smaller,

76
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:46,400
and the idea of self-propelled engines, locomotives, became a conceptual possibility.

77
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:52,140
In 1829, a self-taught mining engineer named George Stevenson

78
00:05:52,141 --> 00:05:55,240
turned a conceptual possibility into a reality

79
00:05:55,241 --> 00:06:00,160
by creating what would become the standard for steam locomotives around the world.

80
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,860
It was called the rocket.

81
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:08,260
The rocket had a smokestack in the front and a boiler with a firebox in the back.

82
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:13,280
The steam it generated would cause pistons to move back and forth in cylinders,

83
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:16,580
which were connected to crank pins on the driving wheels.

84
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,980
The rocket could pull dozens of railroad cars weighing hundreds of tons

85
00:06:23,981 --> 00:06:27,760
at the unheard-of speed of 20 to 25 miles an hour.

86
00:06:27,761 --> 00:06:34,660
It was the first reliable, long-distance, all-weather form of mechanical land transportation,

87
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:40,240
and it had a transforming effect on England and the rest of the world.

88
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:45,780
But the new trains after the rocket were far from being the most reliable form of transportation,

89
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:48,900
or the safest, in Europe or the United States.

90
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:56,440
By the 1840s, locomotive-pulled trains were routinely traveling at 60 and 70 miles an hour

91
00:06:56,441 --> 00:06:58,300
on some railroad lines in the United States.

92
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,780
This was in an era of primitive braking,

93
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:05,620
fragile wooden cars, poorly constructed,

94
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:07,300
poorly maintained track.

95
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:10,460
The consequences of any accident

96
00:07:10,461 --> 00:07:11,960
were generally catastrophic.

97
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:18,900
Bridges would burn down that no one would know,

98
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:21,100
and trains would hurtle off them in the dark.

99
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:24,160
Iron bridges would collapse under the weight of trains.

100
00:07:24,161 --> 00:07:34,720
The primitive rail might become detached and spring up to the floor of a car and skewer whatever poor occupant happened to be in the way.

101
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:39,760
For workers on the trains, even the act of slowing down was dangerous.

102
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,260
All braking was done by hand.

103
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:53,760
The job of the brakeman was to physically apply the brakes by generally turning a wheel that tightened the chain that pulled brake shoes against the wheels of the cars.

104
00:07:53,761 --> 00:07:57,900
Well, clearly you couldn't stop the train quickly in an emergency if that was the case.

105
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:05,460
Then in 1869, George Westinghouse introduced the automatic brake system.

106
00:08:06,740 --> 00:08:10,400
His invention used compressed air to apply pressure to brake valves.

107
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:19,260
In the same year, the United States finished building its first transcontinental railroad

108
00:08:19,261 --> 00:08:24,820
On May 10, 1869, and the event ignited a railroad boom.

109
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:36,380
In the short period between 1860 and 1900, the network of tracks in the United States grew from 30,000 miles to 200,000 miles.

110
00:08:36,381 --> 00:08:42,760
The steam railroad, which allowed people for the first time to travel unimaginable distances

111
00:08:42,761 --> 00:08:49,920
at remarkably high rates of speed, made it conceptually possible to imagine going thousands

112
00:08:49,921 --> 00:08:54,200
of miles of filling this vast continent.

113
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,780
Railroad technology continued to improve.

114
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:07,160
And by the First World War, all the pieces that make up the modern railroad were in place.

115
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:18,100
Automatic air brakes, steel rails, telegraphic communications, automatic track signals, and the steel passenger car.

116
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:31,100
By the 1920s, it was commonplace for high-speed, deluxe passenger trains in this country to routinely travel 70 and 80 miles an hour over short distances.

117
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:41,140
By the 1930s, some railroads were running trains routinely at speeds of over 100 miles an hour, safely and efficiently.

118
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:46,120
Steam engines dominated the rails.

119
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:52,920
But in the mid-1930s, a new type of locomotive could be seen streaking across the nation.

120
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:56,280
The diesel-electric engine.

121
00:09:56,281 --> 00:10:00,280
While not necessarily much faster than steam engines,

122
00:10:00,380 --> 00:10:04,220
diesel electrics were more economical.

123
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:07,120
Steam locomotives were very labor intensive.

124
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:10,720
It took perhaps two or three hours of service and maintenance

125
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:14,200
for every operating hour, whereas diesel engines,

126
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:18,640
in many respects, were as reliable and simple as an automobile.

127
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:23,200
You turn the key, you fire them up, and you go.

128
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:26,280
From the beginning, they became industry

129
00:10:26,281 --> 00:10:30,680
favorites and were quickly put to work pulling some of America's most glamorous trains.

130
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:35,820
In spite of this efficiency and flourishing of technology,

131
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:39,520
passenger rail service in the United States began to decline.

132
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:48,080
In 1956, the decline gained momentum after President Dwight D. Eisenhower signed the

133
00:10:48,081 --> 00:10:52,144
National System of Interstate and Defense Highways Act.

134
00:10:52,145 --> 00:10:55,100
The bill would create a network of super

135
00:10:55,101 --> 00:11:03,080
highways 40,000 miles long. Railroads were left to fend for themselves and as

136
00:11:03,081 --> 00:11:08,480
more Americans turned to their cars into air travel, rail companies shifted their

137
00:11:08,481 --> 00:11:13,400
focus to the more lucrative end of their business, freight service, leaving

138
00:11:13,401 --> 00:11:20,820
passenger service to languish. And finally in 1971 the government struck a deal

139
00:11:20,821 --> 00:11:24,140
with the freight railroads to assume all responsibility for intercity

140
00:11:24,141 --> 00:11:25,980
passenger service.

141
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:27,840
And that was the start of the current system,

142
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:31,180
where you have Amtrak as the single national provider

143
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:32,440
of inner city rail service.

144
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:37,880
On the day Amtrak started operation,

145
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:40,060
it cut the number of daily passenger trains

146
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,760
to fewer than 200, effectively reducing service

147
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:46,960
from 43,000 miles to 16,000.

148
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:52,800
Other industrialized nations struggled

149
00:11:52,801 --> 00:11:55,200
with what to do with their passenger rail service.

150
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:00,620
And if it weren't for a strong-willed 70-year-old man in

151
00:12:00,621 --> 00:12:05,160
Japan named Shinji Sogo, the world's first bullet train

152
00:12:05,161 --> 00:12:06,340
might never have been born.

153
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:13,740
Few trains have captured the imagination of the world,

154
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:15,460
like Japan's bullet train.

155
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:19,280
These streamlined and futuristic-looking passenger

156
00:12:19,281 --> 00:12:22,000
trains race from one end of Japan to the other,

157
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:27,300
at speeds ranging from 130 to 186 miles an hour.

158
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:32,180
They carry more than 130 million passengers each year.

159
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:41,660
Ironically, the country with one of the most advanced train systems in the world today

160
00:12:41,661 --> 00:12:45,160
was one of the last industrialized nations to have a railroad.

161
00:12:45,161 --> 00:12:55,280
Up until the mid-19th century, no one in Japan had ever seen a train.

162
00:12:56,420 --> 00:13:02,340
It was only when America's Commodore Perry returned to Japan in 1854 for a second visit

163
00:13:02,341 --> 00:13:04,920
that the Japanese got their first glimpse.

164
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:11,460
Among the gifts that he brought was a toy train,

165
00:13:11,461 --> 00:13:15,680
and the Japanese had never seen anything like it,

166
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:18,780
and they were fascinated by the train.

167
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:23,820
Japan fully embraced Victorian locomotives and rail technology.

168
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:30,760
From 1864 to 1900, railroads were built throughout Japan

169
00:13:30,761 --> 00:13:33,420
and soon linked most major cities.

170
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:36,440
By the early 20th century,

171
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:39,600
the government agency overseeing Japan's railroads

172
00:13:39,601 --> 00:13:41,440
was the country's largest employer.

173
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:49,180
In 1909, that agency hired graduate student Shinji Sogo.

174
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:53,060
He started out as a minor office clerk,

175
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:56,460
but he would later be known as the father of the bullet train.

176
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:03,900
Sogo was born in 1884 in a small village 31 years

177
00:14:03,901 --> 00:14:06,120
after Commodore Perry first landed in Japan.

178
00:14:06,121 --> 00:14:10,120
As a student, he attended some of Japan's best schools.

179
00:14:10,121 --> 00:14:16,120
He was a man of a lot of ideas, ingenious, mischievous.

180
00:14:16,121 --> 00:14:24,120
When he got to Tokyo to college, he realized the opportunity ahead of him, and he really applied himself.

181
00:14:24,121 --> 00:14:30,120
After landing the job with the railroads, Sogo climbed up through the ranks.

182
00:14:30,121 --> 00:14:35,120
By 1930, he was appointed director of the South Manchuria Railway in China.

183
00:14:35,121 --> 00:14:43,091
in China. Japan controlled Manchuria after winning the Russo-Japanese War of 1905.

184
00:14:43,092 --> 00:14:43,792
They

185
00:14:43,793 --> 00:14:47,180
depended on the railroads there for getting food and raw materials back to Japan.

186
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:57,300
But in 1934, Manchuria's political environment became increasingly unstable, and Sogo resigned.

187
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,591
He returned to Japan to work as a civil servant until his retirement in 1948.

188
00:15:03,592 --> 00:15:04,680
His story might

189
00:15:04,681 --> 00:15:09,640
ended there if it weren't for a crisis in Japan's railroads at the end of World War II.

190
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:18,040
The Japanese railroads at the time of the surrender in 1945 were in terrible shape.

191
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:25,760
The country had been at war for almost 12 years. The railroads have been overloaded.

192
00:15:25,761 --> 00:15:26,760
They had not

193
00:15:26,761 --> 00:15:34,520
been maintained. Tracks, bridges and safety equipment had deteriorated.

194
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:37,540
and there were few resources available to repair them.

195
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:40,840
Worse still, there was no highway system to speak of,

196
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:44,540
and Japan relied heavily on trains for transportation.

197
00:15:45,540 --> 00:15:47,360
They were dangerously overcrowded.

198
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:54,580
Railroad conditions continued to worsen until 1955,

199
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:59,660
when Shinji Sogo was asked to take over his head of Japan National Railways.

200
00:16:00,040 --> 00:16:01,540
The decision surprised the nation.

201
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:03,360
He was 70 years old.

202
00:16:04,260 --> 00:16:08,900
But Sogo had a vision for Japan's railroads and was determined to see it through.

203
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:17,340
His chief idea was to build what was called the Shinkansen,

204
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:22,660
which literally means the new rail line.

205
00:16:22,661 --> 00:16:29,860
He felt that there was a need for an efficient, high-speed rail line

206
00:16:29,861 --> 00:16:35,260
between the two large cities of Osaka and Tokyo.

207
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:39,400
The new line would cover a distance of 320 miles and be

208
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:43,100
completely dedicated to passenger service.

209
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:49,120
The cost of Sogo's plan would still be astronomical.

210
00:16:49,220 --> 00:16:54,160
Land in Japan is very, very scarce, very, very expensive.

211
00:16:54,260 --> 00:16:59,060
They also had to develop a new technology, build new cars, build

212
00:16:59,061 --> 00:17:03,880
an electrical system that would provide the power for this kind of a line.

213
00:17:04,620 --> 00:17:09,000
And so he knew that he would have a very difficult time

214
00:17:09,001 --> 00:17:11,600
getting an appropriation from the government.

215
00:17:13,420 --> 00:17:16,800
To push the project forward, Sogo did two things.

216
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:21,220
He gave a false, low-cost estimate to win government support.

217
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:25,340
And then he did what most entrepreneurs do, got a loan.

218
00:17:25,341 --> 00:17:32,640
Sogo convinced the World Bank that Japan was still a developing nation, and he received $80 million.

219
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:38,300
In the meantime, Sogo's engineers went to work.

220
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:47,220
What they did is they took proven technology being used in Japan and essentially spruced it up and put a new image on it.

221
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:52,340
The trains are what we call electrical multiple unit trains.

222
00:17:52,341 --> 00:17:56,240
they're not that much different than what you'd find in any electrified

223
00:17:56,241 --> 00:18:01,940
commuter railway. They put bigger traction motors and better running gear

224
00:18:01,941 --> 00:18:05,940
under there for the higher speeds, different gearing.

225
00:18:05,941 --> 00:18:11,840
On most trains, heavy traction motors are found in the lead car. What engineers

226
00:18:11,841 --> 00:18:16,180
in Japan did was distribute the traction motors throughout the entire train,

227
00:18:16,181 --> 00:18:18,180
cutting down the weight in front.

228
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:24,180
The weight distribution tactic of putting motors in every car

229
00:18:24,181 --> 00:18:27,180
also gave the train a high horsepower to weight ratio.

230
00:18:30,180 --> 00:18:33,180
This made the Shinkansen very fast.

231
00:18:33,181 --> 00:18:37,180
It would remain a key design feature in all Japanese high-speed trains to follow.

232
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:42,180
This in every step of development was methodical and pragmatic.

233
00:18:42,181 --> 00:18:44,180
This was not a research program.

234
00:18:44,181 --> 00:18:47,180
This was a viable transportation project

235
00:18:47,181 --> 00:18:51,180
to solve a circulation and transportation needs.

236
00:18:51,181 --> 00:18:55,180
They built it fast, and they didn't want to take any risk.

237
00:18:55,181 --> 00:18:57,180
But they were smart, and they went out and hired

238
00:18:57,181 --> 00:19:00,180
a good industrial designer to put a nose on this train,

239
00:19:00,181 --> 00:19:04,180
and hence the term bullet train became the legacy

240
00:19:04,181 --> 00:19:06,180
of the railway business.

241
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:11,180
The bullet train was officially launched

242
00:19:11,181 --> 00:19:17,158
officially launched on October 1964. It was an instant success.

243
00:19:17,159 --> 00:19:20,100
Everybody wanted bullet trains,

244
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:24,800
and that's truly probably the only train that looked like a bullet, but everything that's come

245
00:19:24,801 --> 00:19:27,883
after that is still called a bullet train.

246
00:19:27,884 --> 00:19:31,260
And they basically pushed their envelope from,

247
00:19:31,261 --> 00:19:38,778
let's say, 90 miles an hour to 120. It was very high speed for 1964.

248
00:19:38,779 --> 00:19:41,100
It created a fantasy,

249
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:43,500
that all these other countries, the United States included,

250
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:44,840
said, we have to do that too.

251
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:51,980
In 1981, Sogo died of pneumonia at age 97.

252
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:53,840
But before his death,

253
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:56,980
he received Japan's highest civilian award.

254
00:20:01,260 --> 00:20:04,600
Since the first Tokyo-Osaka line in 1964,

255
00:20:05,480 --> 00:20:08,240
Japan has continually added to its high-speed network

256
00:20:08,241 --> 00:20:11,120
and replaced its old trains with new.

257
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:16,020
Today, there are more than 1,000 miles of high-speed lines

258
00:20:16,021 --> 00:20:22,600
and 360 bullet trains that serve 350,000 passengers each day.

259
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:27,840
They're now up over 200 miles an hour in some of the sections.

260
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:31,760
They've gone to more higher horsepower train sets.

261
00:20:32,740 --> 00:20:36,520
The newest trains are very space-age in that they've been designed

262
00:20:36,521 --> 00:20:38,980
for the minimum aerodynamic drag.

263
00:20:39,980 --> 00:20:41,740
Some people say that this can't be real,

264
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:43,060
but they're out there and running.

265
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:49,480
They have carried more than 2.8 billion passengers

266
00:20:49,481 --> 00:20:51,120
without a single fatality.

267
00:20:51,980 --> 00:20:54,980
There are no grade crossings or extreme curves.

268
00:20:55,640 --> 00:20:58,420
Both measures reduce the chance of collision or derailment.

269
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:01,740
Furthermore, unlike America's high-speed Acela,

270
00:21:02,120 --> 00:21:04,480
it does not share its tracks with slower freight

271
00:21:04,481 --> 00:21:05,560
or passenger trains.

272
00:21:06,520 --> 00:21:15,380
There's also an army of several hundred workers inspecting the length of the track each night.

273
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:18,900
Overhead wires are checked and track is replaced.

274
00:21:18,901 --> 00:21:21,877
And every 10 days, a train called Dr.

275
00:21:21,878 --> 00:21:25,900
Yellow runs diagnostic tests on both rail and wire

276
00:21:25,901 --> 00:21:28,220
using electronic monitoring equipment.

277
00:21:30,680 --> 00:21:35,440
One of the newest trains in service is the Nozami Series 700 train.

278
00:21:36,520 --> 00:21:44,080
Nozami's operating speed is about 177 miles per hour, but it's capable of reaching 200.

279
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:50,520
It's aerodynamically designed with a flattened nose that actually prevents the train from lifting up.

280
00:21:52,700 --> 00:22:00,980
At 708 tons and made with a lightweight aluminum alloy, the Nozami is Japan's lightest train yet.

281
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:05,580
But as light and swift as the Nozami is, there's something faster.

282
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:14,200
It's called the TGV, or train a grand vitesse.

283
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:19,340
It's French for high-speed train, and in any language, it moves.

284
00:22:21,980 --> 00:22:27,820
The fastest conventional train in the world today is a TGV, or train a grand vitesse.

285
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:35,240
In 1990, the TGV Atlantique broke all records by reaching 320 miles an hour.

286
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:45,520
The speed of a regularly scheduled TGV is considerably slower, but the train still turns

287
00:22:45,521 --> 00:22:55,520
heads when flying through the countryside at a cruising speed of 186 miles an hour.

288
00:22:55,521 --> 00:23:04,026
The TGV was introduced in 1981 by the French National Railways, or SNCF.

289
00:23:04,027 --> 00:23:05,680
The trains are

290
00:23:05,681 --> 00:23:11,460
here today because of steps taken in the 1960s by the agency to head off a crisis in the French

291
00:23:11,461 --> 00:23:15,800
railroad industry. The French railroads were condemned.

292
00:23:15,801 --> 00:23:18,720
I mean, if you don't improve yourself

293
00:23:18,721 --> 00:23:25,000
at a time where you have a big expansion of road and air travel, you just disappear.

294
00:23:26,140 --> 00:23:31,360
Unlike passenger trains in the United States, French trains were still one of the most popular

295
00:23:31,361 --> 00:23:33,732
forms of transportation in France.

296
00:23:33,733 --> 00:23:37,640
But ridership on lines between the largest cities was at

297
00:23:37,641 --> 00:23:43,080
the saturation point. In addition, train cars were decades old.

298
00:23:43,081 --> 00:23:49,000
So for the SNCF management, it was quite a challenge, not only to manage the day-to-day

299
00:23:49,001 --> 00:23:56,840
business, but also to come up with a vision for the future that would be compelling and

300
00:23:56,841 --> 00:23:57,840
exciting enough.

301
00:23:57,841 --> 00:24:03,160
Remember, in the 60s, Concorde was being built.

302
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:06,900
There was a great expansion of the highways in France.

303
00:24:07,620 --> 00:24:11,580
And you want the railroads not to do anything to react about that?

304
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:13,280
They have to invent something.

305
00:24:14,160 --> 00:24:16,340
One idea was the aero train.

306
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:21,460
It was a jet-powered train that ran on a cushion of air, much like a hovercraft.

307
00:24:21,461 --> 00:24:29,660
In experiments, it had reached speeds over 100 miles an hour and had a great deal of

308
00:24:29,661 --> 00:24:31,460
support because it was futuristic.

309
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:36,120
But it was the less exotic turbo trains that won out.

310
00:24:37,780 --> 00:24:41,980
French engineers were certain they could create a train with even greater speed and performance.

311
00:24:41,981 --> 00:24:47,680
And in early 1970, they developed the TGV-001.

312
00:24:47,681 --> 00:24:57,580
In 1972, powered by a gas turbine, the TGV 001 set a world record with the incredible

313
00:24:57,581 --> 00:25:00,460
speed of 198 miles an hour.

314
00:25:00,461 --> 00:25:04,640
The TGV was an absolute revolution.

315
00:25:04,641 --> 00:25:13,320
It was the end result of the ability to master technology to a point that was not conceivable

316
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:16,000
in most minds before.

317
00:25:16,001 --> 00:25:23,000
Engineers had created a train that was light in weight and harnessed it to a powerful engine.

318
00:25:23,001 --> 00:25:29,000
To cut down on tonnage, they reduced the number of axles.

319
00:25:29,001 --> 00:25:33,000
Most conventional trains have a pair of two axle trucks per car.

320
00:25:33,001 --> 00:25:36,000
On a TGV, the axles are shared.

321
00:25:36,001 --> 00:25:40,000
The ends of two cars rest on a common axle truck.

322
00:25:40,001 --> 00:25:44,000
Aerodynamics are improved due to the lower weight of the train

323
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:50,220
weight of the train and smaller gaps between cars.

324
00:25:50,221 --> 00:25:53,680
This design is present in all TGVs.

325
00:25:53,681 --> 00:26:03,380
But the global oil crisis of 1973 suddenly made turbo TGVs very costly.

326
00:26:03,381 --> 00:26:09,680
During the same year, the TGV became electric, and like the bullet train in Japan, the TGV

327
00:26:09,681 --> 00:26:12,760
would obtain its power from overhead wires.

328
00:26:12,761 --> 00:26:20,400
In order to exploit the technology possibilities of the TGV concept, there was a need to build new tracks.

329
00:26:25,640 --> 00:26:35,720
The SNCF chose to build a dedicated high-speed route 256 miles long between Paris and France's second-largest city, Lyon.

330
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:39,580
Construction began in 1976.

331
00:26:39,581 --> 00:26:45,500
costs would be kept down because the new line would use existing tracks for the first 18 miles

332
00:26:45,501 --> 00:26:51,260
outside of Paris and share the infrastructure of two roadways, eliminating the need for extensive

333
00:26:51,261 --> 00:26:51,961
excavation.

334
00:26:51,962 --> 00:26:56,400
The rest of the route would be a straight shot, crossing vast open stretches of

335
00:26:56,401 --> 00:26:58,132
countryside.

336
00:26:58,133 --> 00:27:09,100
The first section of high-speed rail between Paris and Lyon opened September

337
00:27:09,101 --> 00:27:17,020
27, 1981, and TGVs painted bright orange were soon running 164 miles an hour along its rails.

338
00:27:18,700 --> 00:27:20,380
They won rave reviews.

339
00:27:23,820 --> 00:27:29,345
I think that train had in itself such an emotional content.

340
00:27:29,346 --> 00:27:32,060
It has definitely led the way

341
00:27:33,100 --> 00:27:36,460
for many other projects, not only in France, but also in Europe.

342
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:42,100
Today, France has the world's most extensive network

343
00:27:42,101 --> 00:27:45,100
of high-speed rail in the world.

344
00:27:45,101 --> 00:27:48,100
It's a country about the size of Texas,

345
00:27:48,101 --> 00:27:51,100
but has nearly 1,000 miles of high-speed traffic,

346
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:53,100
and its network is growing.

347
00:27:53,101 --> 00:27:56,100
Success has been astounding.

348
00:27:56,101 --> 00:28:00,100
And with the link from Paris to the Channel Tunnel toward England,

349
00:28:00,101 --> 00:28:04,100
they now have high-speed rail from coast of the English Channel

350
00:28:04,101 --> 00:28:07,100
to coast on the Mediterranean.

351
00:28:07,101 --> 00:28:10,100
TGVs have also proven to be safe.

352
00:28:10,101 --> 00:28:14,100
After more than 20 years of operation and over 17 million

353
00:28:14,101 --> 00:28:17,100
riders a year, there have been no fatalities.

354
00:28:20,100 --> 00:28:25,100
So successful, they've spawned an entire industry in France.

355
00:28:25,101 --> 00:28:30,100
From a workshop outside of Paris that provides daily maintenance,

356
00:28:30,101 --> 00:28:35,100
to the Alstom factory in the coastal town of La Rochelle

357
00:28:35,101 --> 00:28:37,840
that manufactures double-decker TGVs.

358
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:41,880
You cannot fault the French for not doing a fine job,

359
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:44,900
a quality job, in putting out a product

360
00:28:44,901 --> 00:28:48,640
that's been basically one of the models of the world.

361
00:28:49,980 --> 00:28:52,760
And if you ever want to sell a train system here,

362
00:28:52,820 --> 00:28:54,840
you point to France and say, we ought to have that too.

363
00:28:56,600 --> 00:28:59,680
And while today's high-speed trains like the TGV

364
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:01,900
provide the fastest rail service in the world,

365
00:29:01,901 --> 00:29:08,340
There are many who believe there's a quicker way for trains to travel, one that doesn't

366
00:29:08,341 --> 00:29:18,660
involve steel wheels or rail.

367
00:29:18,661 --> 00:29:25,880
On April 14, 1999, an experimental train in Japan called the MLX-01 smashed a world

368
00:29:25,881 --> 00:29:27,840
speed record.

369
00:29:27,841 --> 00:29:30,980
at an astonishing 345 miles per hour,

370
00:29:31,540 --> 00:29:33,100
no wheels guided its movement,

371
00:29:33,540 --> 00:29:35,220
and no engine powered its cars.

372
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:38,060
It moved entirely by magnetic force.

373
00:29:38,680 --> 00:29:40,720
The train is known as a maglev.

374
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:45,320
There are no maglevs in commercial service in the world today,

375
00:29:45,820 --> 00:29:48,720
but Japan, China, and even companies in Germany

376
00:29:48,721 --> 00:29:51,020
and the United States hope to change that.

377
00:29:52,600 --> 00:29:54,880
The idea behind maglevs is simple.

378
00:29:54,881 --> 00:29:59,560
Put two attracting magnets near each other and they clamp together.

379
00:30:00,280 --> 00:30:03,360
Try the same thing with two repelling magnets and they push apart.

380
00:30:04,260 --> 00:30:09,100
This principle is used to levitate trains and guide trains above a special track.

381
00:30:12,580 --> 00:30:18,100
Maglevs run on a special guideway lined with magnets that change polarity from north to south.

382
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:24,800
The maglev is propelled forward as magnets in the guideway attract and repel magnets located on the train.

383
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:31,680
It's an incredibly quiet ride because you don't really hear them go by.

384
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:38,260
You hear the rush of the wind, but since there's no moving motor, it's all magnetic,

385
00:30:38,720 --> 00:30:40,620
all you hear is the aerodynamic noise.

386
00:30:41,160 --> 00:30:44,760
There is no friction, there is no contact, you know, on the infrastructure.

387
00:30:45,880 --> 00:30:51,320
So there are a lot of benefits at first, you know, around the concept.

388
00:30:51,321 --> 00:31:01,400
The only issue I personally see with the MagLife is it is absolutely not compatible with anything that exists today.

389
00:31:02,500 --> 00:31:05,940
The idea that it is so different has attracted supporters.

390
00:31:06,760 --> 00:31:10,560
It may be the first new transportation technology in 100 years.

391
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:20,540
There were some inventions in this country and ideas that magnetic forces can be used for accelerating and powering the vehicles.

392
00:31:20,541 --> 00:31:27,640
But it was really Germany in the 1970s that began to apply that and to build magnetic levitation vehicles.

393
00:31:28,900 --> 00:31:36,000
Today, a German company called TransRapid conducts maglev studies on this 20-mile track in Emsland, West Germany.

394
00:31:37,180 --> 00:31:43,460
The levitation magnets on the TransRapid system are located on the train's wraparound arms and on the guideway.

395
00:31:44,060 --> 00:31:45,580
These magnets propel the train.

396
00:31:46,020 --> 00:31:48,760
They also keep the train suspended with magnets on the side.

397
00:31:49,380 --> 00:31:53,760
Together they keep the train at a uniform distance of 10 millimeters from the guideway.

398
00:31:58,500 --> 00:32:03,800
Transrapid engineers have run the train to speeds of up to 271 miles per hour.

399
00:32:05,300 --> 00:32:09,200
They claim it could go even faster if it weren't for the short distance of their track.

400
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:15,920
Germany has poured more than a billion dollars into the Transrapid program,

401
00:32:15,921 --> 00:32:20,760
But the country recently scrapped plans to build a maglev between Berlin and Hamburg.

402
00:32:21,880 --> 00:32:27,680
The cost of the planned 185-mile-long guideway was cited as being too high.

403
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:33,720
If you try and do something exotic that's different, that's not compatible with what

404
00:32:33,721 --> 00:32:37,680
you've got, you've got to spend humongous amounts of money because you have to build

405
00:32:37,681 --> 00:32:38,381
it all.

406
00:32:38,382 --> 00:32:39,560
That's one of the issues with maglev.

407
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:45,800
You have to build the entire system out to get where you want to go.

408
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:52,920
In 2001, TransRapid exported its maglev technology to China.

409
00:32:52,921 --> 00:32:58,920
The government plans to build a maglev line that will carry 600 travelers 18 miles from

410
00:32:58,921 --> 00:33:02,920
the Shanghai airport to its financial district.

411
00:33:02,921 --> 00:33:08,920
The trip is scheduled to take just seven minutes on trains that will run over 200 miles an hour.

412
00:33:08,921 --> 00:33:13,520
Shanghai could see its first commercial maglev up and running by 2003.

413
00:33:17,780 --> 00:33:22,900
After nearly 30 years of research, Japan is still working to perfect its maglev.

414
00:33:24,440 --> 00:33:28,700
Today's experiments are conducted on this 27-mile track west of Tokyo.

415
00:33:30,520 --> 00:33:34,500
The testing that's scheduled there is expected to take several more years.

416
00:33:34,501 --> 00:33:41,060
In the U.S., however, a maglev system could be operating much earlier.

417
00:33:43,300 --> 00:33:47,360
A company called American Maglev broke ground on a new maglev system

418
00:33:47,361 --> 00:33:52,220
that will carry students across campus at Old Dominion University in Norfolk, Virginia.

419
00:33:54,980 --> 00:33:57,880
As many as 100 students will board this vehicle.

420
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:03,580
It will travel a distance of about a mile and complete its journey in 2 minutes and 3 seconds.

421
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:11,500
And there could be more maglev projects here in the United States.

422
00:34:11,501 --> 00:34:15,220
Cities are vying for development money from the Department of Transportation to develop

423
00:34:15,221 --> 00:34:17,700
their own maglev systems.

424
00:34:17,701 --> 00:34:25,260
We were given the authority and some funds to begin choosing places where a maglev demonstration

425
00:34:25,261 --> 00:34:27,260
project would actually work.

426
00:34:27,261 --> 00:34:31,500
The two sites that have been chosen for further development

427
00:34:31,501 --> 00:34:36,000
are Baltimore, Washington, and Pittsburgh.

428
00:34:39,940 --> 00:34:42,900
But critics believe that maglevs offer few advantages

429
00:34:42,901 --> 00:34:46,080
over high-speed rail trains.

430
00:34:46,081 --> 00:34:50,180
The main image that maglev has is that it is much higher speed

431
00:34:50,181 --> 00:34:54,480
than conventional rail and high-speed rail.

432
00:34:54,481 --> 00:34:56,860
That used to be true maybe 30 years ago

433
00:34:56,861 --> 00:35:01,300
when railroads were traveling up to 100 miles per hour only.

434
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:04,100
Now, with the advancement of railroads,

435
00:35:04,200 --> 00:35:08,200
the difference between the two is really very small.

436
00:35:08,300 --> 00:35:11,680
The future of maglev development remains uncertain,

437
00:35:11,780 --> 00:35:15,180
but the quest for better passenger travel continues,

438
00:35:15,280 --> 00:35:18,640
and tomorrow's trains might well travel under oceans

439
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:20,720
and link our hemispheres.

440
00:35:20,721 --> 00:35:30,160
The 21st century has arrived, and futurists are pondering what form the next generation

441
00:35:30,161 --> 00:35:34,460
of high-speed trains will take.

442
00:35:34,461 --> 00:35:40,380
In Switzerland, plans are underway to develop what's called the Swiss Metro Project.

443
00:35:40,381 --> 00:35:47,220
The idea is to propel pressurized levitation vehicles at 250 miles an hour in tube tunnels

444
00:35:47,221 --> 00:35:50,120
located deep in the earth.

445
00:35:50,121 --> 00:35:56,320
No layer in the tunnels is removed to create a partial vacuum.

446
00:35:56,321 --> 00:36:03,240
The vehicle will be propelled by a system similar to a maglev.

447
00:36:03,241 --> 00:36:09,440
Electromagnets will be used to levitate and guide it.

448
00:36:09,441 --> 00:36:11,240
Switching the trains to different tunnels

449
00:36:11,241 --> 00:36:17,120
will be achieved by a mechanism similar to the chamber of a gun.

450
00:36:17,121 --> 00:36:21,660
The creators of Swiss Metro envision a system of tunnels linking all its major cities,

451
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:24,140
and eventually, all of Europe.

452
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:27,000
They also believe it could be used to link continents.

453
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:33,720
A preliminary study shows that the Swiss Metro is technically feasible and economically viable,

454
00:36:34,080 --> 00:36:38,340
and backers hope to link at least two cities by 2010.

455
00:36:39,880 --> 00:36:44,900
But those who follow the industry predict that the train of the future will be a little less exotic.

456
00:36:47,120 --> 00:36:51,420
Because building a new rail infrastructure is so costly,

457
00:36:52,060 --> 00:36:56,880
today's trend is to get high-speed or high-performance trains on non-high-speed rail.

458
00:37:01,080 --> 00:37:05,320
France's TGVs are capable of running on both AC and DC power,

459
00:37:05,940 --> 00:37:08,200
as well as three different electrical systems,

460
00:37:08,620 --> 00:37:13,240
so that they can run on other older, non-high-speed lines that are electrified.

461
00:37:13,241 --> 00:37:22,240
Japan has imported high-speed diesel trains from England to run on its older non-electrified routes.

462
00:37:26,240 --> 00:37:33,240
In the United States, efforts are also underway to get faster trains on slower non-electrified rail lines.

463
00:37:33,241 --> 00:37:42,500
at the transportation technology center in pueblo colorado tests were conducted on a turbine electric

464
00:37:42,501 --> 00:37:48,600
train designed by the canadian manufacturer bombardier the train is powered by a jet engine

465
00:37:48,601 --> 00:37:57,020
turbine and is capable of operating at 150 miles per hour that performance puts it in the company

466
00:37:57,021 --> 00:37:59,040
of many of today's high-speed trains.

467
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:03,080
The turbine engines that are out there

468
00:38:03,081 --> 00:38:05,740
are putting out much more power

469
00:38:05,741 --> 00:38:09,700
with much less fuel use and pollution.

470
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:12,480
That has a lot of appeal for urban areas

471
00:38:12,481 --> 00:38:15,460
that are looking at these kind of inner-city travel.

472
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:19,240
The states of Florida, California, Michigan, and others

473
00:38:19,241 --> 00:38:22,040
are all working to develop their own high-speed lines.

474
00:38:22,041 --> 00:38:28,120
Many states are looking at any variety of options.

475
00:38:28,840 --> 00:38:31,500
There are some places that maglev is going to work.

476
00:38:31,940 --> 00:38:36,480
There are other places where an electrified system would make more sense.

477
00:38:36,481 --> 00:38:42,280
And then there are other places in the country where a non-electric version would probably make more sense.

478
00:38:46,080 --> 00:38:49,860
European companies hope to build trains for this new American market.

479
00:38:49,861 --> 00:38:54,340
Bombardier and French company Alstom collaborated on the Acela.

480
00:38:56,980 --> 00:39:01,920
And now the manufacturers Talgo in Spain and Siemens,

481
00:39:02,320 --> 00:39:06,500
builders of Germany's trains, are competing to build other

482
00:39:06,501 --> 00:39:08,220
high-speed trains in this country.

483
00:39:09,560 --> 00:39:14,480
Whether the high-speed train of the future is electric or turbine,

484
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:19,620
diesel, or propelled by magnets,

485
00:39:19,860 --> 00:39:23,860
It will be designed to wow and woo customers.

486
00:39:23,861 --> 00:39:26,860
In a long distance train or a high speed train, it's very different.

487
00:39:26,861 --> 00:39:31,860
It should be a richer experience with perhaps more textures or more color.

488
00:39:31,861 --> 00:39:36,860
I go after creating an environment that will really inspire you

489
00:39:36,861 --> 00:39:40,860
and you're going to want to tell your friends, you should ride that train.

490
00:39:40,861 --> 00:39:44,860
The French with the TGV have always known the importance of design

491
00:39:44,861 --> 00:39:47,860
and have used it to market their trains.

492
00:39:47,861 --> 00:39:53,240
When you board a TGV today, you feel like you're not boarding any train.

493
00:39:53,740 --> 00:39:55,960
You're boarding a very, very different type of train.

494
00:39:56,840 --> 00:40:04,200
So it's hard to explain, but I think it is in a way at the core also of the success of the product.

495
00:40:05,580 --> 00:40:06,960
Japan is no different.

496
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:14,800
From the first Bullitt train in 1964 to the Nozami 700 series, their trains convey speed and efficiency.

497
00:40:14,801 --> 00:40:23,660
Allstom's AGV is a new train that hasn't yet hit the market.

498
00:40:24,840 --> 00:40:27,580
It was designed not only for high-speed performance,

499
00:40:28,020 --> 00:40:30,720
but also to promote an image of speed and comfort.

500
00:40:33,360 --> 00:40:35,140
Railroads have been with us a long time,

501
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:37,360
probably be here a long time to come.

502
00:40:38,580 --> 00:40:40,060
Will they look different? Most likely.

503
00:40:40,460 --> 00:40:42,120
But the basic need is still there.

504
00:40:42,121 --> 00:40:44,500
It's a seat that's environmentally comfortable.

505
00:40:44,501 --> 00:40:48,620
heating, cooling, some degree of food service if the trip is long enough.

506
00:40:48,740 --> 00:40:49,960
These are basic comforts.

507
00:40:50,300 --> 00:40:53,920
The transportation system that meets those needs will be a success.

508
00:40:55,140 --> 00:40:58,300
For the high-speed rail industry in Europe and Japan,

509
00:40:58,880 --> 00:41:01,660
it used to be all about improving travel time.

510
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:04,580
Today's speed is no longer the focus.

511
00:41:05,420 --> 00:41:07,860
Like the automobile and passenger jet industries,

512
00:41:08,580 --> 00:41:11,600
train companies are focusing on fine-tuning their products,

513
00:41:11,740 --> 00:41:14,160
making them more cost-efficient and safer.

514
00:41:14,500 --> 00:41:18,160
In fact, many believe there is a point when fast is too fast.

515
00:41:18,760 --> 00:41:22,380
It's very exciting to be able to go faster and even faster.

516
00:41:22,640 --> 00:41:27,800
But there will be a point where certain speeds are unreasonable

517
00:41:27,801 --> 00:41:32,860
because the communities that you're serving are too close for that speed,

518
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:35,340
for the ride to be worth the ride.

519
00:41:36,320 --> 00:41:38,520
There's also a financial consideration.

520
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:44,280
The cost of maintenance on both train and track significantly rises with speed.

521
00:41:44,500 --> 00:41:53,500
But as manufacturers and designers work through these problems, the number of high-speed train

522
00:41:53,501 --> 00:41:58,220
networks will continue to expand. Europe and Asia show tremendous growth.

523
00:41:59,940 --> 00:42:04,677
What will happen in the United States is still up for speculation.

524
00:42:04,678 --> 00:42:06,400
We can only assume that,

525
00:42:06,500 --> 00:42:11,040
like today's bullet trains, trains of the future in America and the rest of the world

526
00:42:11,041 --> 00:42:14,600
will get us to our destination safely, in comfort,

527
00:42:15,120 --> 00:42:17,420
and faster than we ever dreamed possible.

528
00:42:27,460 --> 00:42:29,300
There are always going to be surprises.

529
00:42:30,820 --> 00:42:33,980
You don't have to speak the language to be able to interact with people.

530
00:42:35,400 --> 00:42:38,780
Knowing that can make you look at history in a new and exciting way.

